---
title: "What is QQL?"
description: "Use one readable query language for Qdrant retrieval, data operations, schema work, and policy-safe execution."
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

QQL gives a product team one query surface for search, retrieval pipelines,
point mutations, and collection management. The same source works in a Rust
service, Python worker, Node.js API, browser tool, or the `qql` CLI.

{% grid style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap:1rem;" %}

{% glassCard title="Readable operations" %}
Keep the intent, filter, vector choice, and result shape together instead of
assembling transport-specific request bodies across application layers.
{% /glassCard %}

{% glassCard title="Inspectable before execution" %}
Parse, explain, and compile a statement locally. The playground exposes the
same AST and Qdrant route your application will use.
{% /glassCard %}

{% glassCard title="Portable integration" %}
Adopt QQL in the host language that already owns your product workflow rather
than running a separate query service just to translate syntax.
{% /glassCard %}

{% glassCard title="Policy at the source" %}
Inject a trusted predicate into the parsed AST before planning. Tenant filtering
is part of the operation, not a post-processing step.
{% /glassCard %}

{% /grid %}

## Where QQL fits

Choose QQL when the product benefits from reviewable query source, consistent
retrieval behavior across services, or policy enforcement before Qdrant sees a
request. Use a native Qdrant SDK directly when your application already owns a
stable typed request builder and portability is not useful.

{% qqlExample title="Hybrid retrieval scoped to one service" %}
```sql
QUERY TEXT 'incident response'
FROM runbooks
USING HYBRID
  DENSE dense
  SPARSE bm25
  FUSION RRF
WHERE service = 'payments'
LIMIT 8;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

## Two boundaries to remember

{% aside type="caution" title="Isolation is not routing" %}
`WHERE tenant_id = 'acme'` or host-side `inject_filter` is the security
boundary. `SHARD 'acme'` only routes the request to a custom shard key.
Production multi-tenant systems commonly use both.
{% /aside %}

{% aside type="note" title="Parsing is not execution" %}
Parsing proves complete QQL syntax. Planning can still require collection
topology, and execution can still require an embedder or a live Qdrant backend.
{% /aside %}

Continue with [installation](/docs/getting-started/installation/) or run the
[quickstart](/docs/getting-started/quickstart/).
